#
# RESOLVED (July 2026, milestone zero) — kept as a regression note.
#
# The problem was: the word "enter" carried three roles — a verb,
# a key name, and a key token — one concept, three spellings, and
# the same action writable three ways:
#
#   enter "exit"        # guest text + the key   (verb)
#   press enter         # just the key           (key name)
#   type  "<enter>"     # also just the key      (key token, OLD SURFACE)
#
# THE FIX: <key> tokens are deleted. Strings carry text and only
# text; keys travel only through press. The \< escape disappeared
# with the tokens (three string escapes remain), and key names now
# appear in exactly one lexical position — after press:

description "The spellings of the Enter key — resolved"
platform    dos
timeout     30s

enter "dir"             # verb: guest text, then the Enter key
press enter             # the key alone
type  "A:"              # text alone; no key markup exists
press enter

# enter remains a derived form — `enter "cls"` is `type "cls"`
# then `press enter` — kept because console lines dominate real
# install scripts (see design-install.rlqs).
#
# RESIDUE (minor, accepted): "enter" is still a verb in one
# position and a key name after press. The two are distinguished
# purely positionally, so a reader never needs context beyond the
# line itself.
